What is CGCalc
==============

CGCalc is a simple double precision calculator similar to xcalc, with
the usual scientific functions: trigonometry in degrees, radians or
grads, logarithms, powers, factorials, parentheses and a memory. A
programmer's keypad works on words of 8 to 64 bits in hexadecimal,
decimal, octal or binary, with the bitwise operations and shifts, and
shows every bit of the word. Run with -analog, it is a slide rule
instead, as the X11R3 xcalc had.

CGCalc is a fork of GCalc, the calculator of gnome-utils 1.4.1.2,
ported from GNOME 1 to plain GTK 1.2. It is renamed because it no
longer belongs to gnome-utils, and GNOME has a calculator of its own.


Building
========

See the README at the top of cgtools.


Use
===

  cgcalc [-analog]


Authors
=======

GCalc was written by George Lebl <jirka@5z.com>. The slide rule is
the X11R3 xcalc's, brought over by Jamie Zawinski <jwz@jwz.org>.
